{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261118T193744Z-bada7a8a", "ts": "2026-11-18T19:41:31Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "fix/1188-git-decode", "build": "3682719168c6"}, "body": {"base_commit": "bd85aa7138003ff69ab4a09b1c505637d81413c0", "base_tree": "fa2b9796b1209b3a47fd95b40160ddc75b64e614", "head_tree": "64b8172e7ee61c0f12b8efbf1c6c71c33aa6f6d1", "head_commit": null, "dispatch_commit": "bd85aa7138003ff69ab4a09b1c505637d81413c0", "mode": "final (full review, ready for push)", "mode_chosen_by": "rule-3 final: no build plan, session diff has 26 changed files (medium+ work \u2014 full review warranted)", "stage": "inner", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/_python_floor.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify", "plugin/hooks/banner.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/buildplan_refs.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_migrate.py", "plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py", "plugin/lib/record_progress.py", "plugin/lib/release_verification.py", "plugin/lib/retired_claims.py", "plugin/lib/review_dispatch.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "plugin/lib/stranded_work.py", "plugin/lib/verdict_cache.py", "tests/test_pr_gate.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py", "tests/test_stranded_work.py", "tests/test_subprocess_decoding.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/changes/subprocess-utf8-decoding.md", "plugin/bin/_python_floor.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify", "plugin/hooks/banner.py", "plugin/lib/backlog/transport.py", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/buildplan_refs.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_migrate.py", "plugin/lib/migrate_plugin.py", "plugin/lib/record_progress.py", "plugin/lib/release_verification.py", "plugin/lib/retired_claims.py", "plugin/lib/review_dispatch.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "plugin/lib/stranded_work.py", "plugin/lib/verdict_cache.py", "tests/test_pr_gate.py", "tests/test_record_lint.py", "tests/test_stranded_work.py", "tests/test_subprocess_decoding.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/changes/subprocess-utf8-decoding.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Strings decoded with surrogateescape now reach strict UTF-8 writers, so a read that used to degrade quietly now crashes session start or loses the handoff, and no test covers it", "recommendation": "Class, not instance. Why it broke: the change made ~80 subprocess reads lossless (a non-UTF-8 byte becomes a lone surrogate such as U+DCE9) but left every downstream encoder strict, and a lone surrogate raises UnicodeEncodeError (a ValueError) at the first strict encode. Before, the failure happened at the read, behind a guard written for it. Two members found by tracing git-derived text to its writers. (1) Session start: `gitstate.git_status_output` used to return None on a non-UTF-8 path, through its broad except. It now returns the text, and `_boundary_capture_git_anchors` (plugin/bin/prawduct-hook ~1458) passes it to `core.atomic_write_text` (open(encoding='utf-8'), strict). That call catches only `(OSError, ImportError)`, so the UnicodeEncodeError escapes `clear --session-start`. The session-base-tree anchor, `_record_session_start`, the ingest and the briefing after it never run, and a partial `.session-git-baseline.tmp` is left behind. The trigger is a dirty or untracked path holding a non-UTF-8 byte in a repo with `core.quotePath=false`, a common setting among non-ASCII users. With the default quotePath=true, porcelain v1 octal-quotes such paths. (2) Handoff: `briefing._git_session_commits` used to return [] when a commit subject had a raw legacy byte (its broad except caught the decode error). It now returns the subject, and the `.session-handoff.md` write in briefing.py (~2356) fails as a whole. The handoff is not written and a NOTE goes to stderr, where before only the commit list was missing. The change record says such content 'now reads it instead of reporting the read as failed', but no writer of git-derived text was audited. To bound the class, search for every strict encoder that can receive subprocess output: `atomic_write_text`, `write_text(`, `open(... 'w'`, `.encode(` without 'surrogateescape', and `json.dumps(..., ensure_ascii=False)`. Expect members beyond these two. A longer list of fixed sites will not close the class. It needs one owner that every member goes through, for example `atomic_write_text` (and the readers of those state files) using surrogateescape, which restores the original bytes and fits these files as mirrors of git output. Add a test that writes a status or commit holding a non-UTF-8 byte through session start and checks that it completes.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/briefing.py"], "fid": "R-1"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 0, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "run_git's `except UnicodeDecodeError` arm is now unreachable, and the mechanical replace left its comment saying surrogateescape 'decodes strictly'", "recommendation": "Instance. The `text=True` to `encoding=..., errors=\"surrogateescape\"` replacement also rewrote the comment inside the except arm in plugin/lib/evidence.py (~1286), which now says the reverse of the truth. Decoding with surrogateescape cannot raise UnicodeDecodeError, so the arm and its 'output is not valid UTF-8' message are dead. Delete the arm and its comment instead of rewording them. The test_stranded_work change already stopped relying on that message.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "test-reference-verify's backslashreplace reconfigure has no test of its own", "recommendation": "The change record says test-reference-verify prints a non-UTF-8 byte as an escape instead of crashing. Only the prawduct-hook copy of that reconfigure is exercised, by test_pr_gate's pathname test. The two copies are separate code, so one could go without a test noticing. A test that runs test-reference-verify over a changed file with a non-UTF-8 name (quotePath off) would pin it.", "files": ["plugin/bin/test-reference-verify"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "run_git keeps an unreachable `except UnicodeDecodeError` branch, and its comment now says surrogateescape 'decodes strictly'", "recommendation": "In plugin/lib/evidence.py run_git, the replacement of `text=True` also rewrote the comment inside the `except UnicodeDecodeError` branch. It now reads \"`encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"surrogateescape\"` decodes strictly\", which contradicts the docstring a few lines above it. The branch can no longer run: surrogateescape never raises on decode, and tests/test_stranded_work.py already stopped faking its 'output is not valid UTF-8' message. A maintainer reading the comment would wrongly assume a decode failure is still possible and might keep or copy the guard. Delete both the branch and its comment rather than rewording them. Scope: one instance. Grepping plugin/ for 'decodes strictly' and 'not valid UTF-8' found no other comment rewritten the same way. This is Goal 7 dead code as well as Goal 4 drift; it is one observation, not two.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The decode policy is spelled out at roughly 65 call sites because about a dozen private git runners duplicate evidence.run_git", "recommendation": "Class, and it has a construction: tests/test_subprocess_decoding.py derives a check from the source (an AST walk over every spawner, which flags ** splats as unreadable and includes positive controls), so the decoding class is closed. What remains is the reason the fix had to touch so many sites. run_git's docstring says callers 'borrow the runner rather than growing their own', yet git_hygiene, pr_gate, evidence_sync, change_fragments, learnings_migrate, learnings_files, risk, record_lint, gitstate (both _run_git_text and _git_text), banner and test-reference-verify each define their own git wrapper, and the next cross-cutting git policy will be paid site by site again. No change is needed in this diff. If it is worth doing, file a backlog item to route library git calls through one runner (or share one kwargs constant). Search for `def _git`, `_git_text`, `_git_lines` and `_run_git` under plugin/.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/risk.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_migrate.py", "plugin/lib/learnings_files.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Mechanical keyword substitution left non-idiomatic formatting: two keywords on one line inside vertical argument lists, and single lines of about 120 characters", "recommendation": "Wherever the original call listed one keyword per line, the substitution now puts `encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"surrogateescape\",` on a single line (gitstate.py, coverage.py, critic_mode.py, briefing.py, prawduct-hook _untrack_session_files and others). Several one-line calls now run to about 120 characters or more (buildplan_refs._changed_on_branch, git_hygiene._gh_json and after_merge, migrate_plugin, verdict_cache, prawduct-hook cmd_stop). Nothing reads this and it changes nothing anyone does. If you touch these sites again, run a formatter pass. No edit is needed now.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gitstate.py", "plugin/lib/buildplan_refs.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The stdout/stderr backslashreplace reconfigure is copied into each entry point's main", "recommendation": "prawduct-hook main() and test-reference-verify main() carry identical four-line reconfigure loops. Two copies is tolerable, and hooks/banner.py does not need one because it decodes with errors=\"replace\". A third entry point that prints git-derived strings would have to remember to add the loop, and nothing would catch it if it forgot. If one is ever added, move the loop to a shared bin helper (for example beside _python_floor.py) at that point. Scope: an instance with two members, both found.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/bin/test-reference-verify"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "run_git's UnicodeDecodeError branch is now unreachable, and its comment now says the opposite of the code", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the `text=True` -> `encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"surrogateescape\"` swap was text-anchored and also matched the token inside the comment (core rule: \"A text-anchored edit changes a NEIGHBORHOOD, not a point\"). The comment at plugin/lib/evidence.py ~L1286 now reads \"`encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"surrogateescape\"` decodes strictly\", which is false, and the `except UnicodeDecodeError` branch it explains can no longer be reached, because surrogateescape never raises on decode. A maintainer reading it will think run_git still fails on non-UTF-8 output and that callers still see \"output is not valid UTF-8\". Delete the branch and its comment. The run_git docstring added in this change already states the real contract. I found no other comment the swap rewrote (`git diff -U0` filtered for added comment lines that mention surrogateescape).", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Surrogate-escaped git text now reaches strict UTF-8 writers: a legacy-encoded commit subject now loses the whole session handoff, not just the commit list", "recommendation": "Class. Why it broke: decoding with surrogateescape moves the failure from the subprocess decode, which every caller handled, to the first strict encode downstream, which none of them expected. One member I traced: briefing._git_session_commits (`git log --oneline`; a commit message with raw non-UTF-8 bytes and no encoding header is output as-is) feeds the handoff text, and briefing.py ~L2356 writes it through core.atomic_write_text, which is strict UTF-8. That raises UnicodeEncodeError on the lone surrogate. The handler catches it and prints 'could not write .session-handoff.md' to stderr, so the failure is detected, not silent. But the whole handoff is lost, where before only the commit list was dropped. The stdout streams of prawduct-hook and test-reference-verify are covered by this change. Its file writers are not. To bound the class, search for git- or gh-derived strings that reach atomic_write_text, Path.write_text or json.dumps(ensure_ascii=False). Closing it needs one owner: a writer of display text that encodes with backslashreplace, mirroring the stream reconfigure. Fixing it call site by call site will not close it. A test could also pin a surrogate-bearing commit subject through cmd_clear's handoff.", "files": ["plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-8"}], "duration_seconds": 600, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/1188-git-decode`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "base_reviewed": null, "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/subprocess-utf8-decoding.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved